<p>
Just a quickie today. Marcin Borkowski (mbork) has a very nice little post on using <kbd>Tab</kbd> with Dired. By default, <kbd>Tab</kbd> isn&rsquo;t defined in Dired but mbork suggests an excellent use for it and provides the code to implement his suggestion.
</p>
<p>
If there are two Dired windows open, the default destination for Dired commands is &ldquo;the other window&rdquo;. That&rsquo;s a handy thing that not every Emacs user knows. Mbork&rsquo;s idea is to use <kbd>Tab</kbd> to switch between Dired windows.
</p>
<p>
It&rsquo;s a small thing, of course, but it&rsquo;s a nice example of reducing friction in your Emacs workflow. As Mbork says, it&rsquo;s yet another example of how easy it is to make small optimizations like this in Emacs.</p>
